      "name": "problem_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query by categorising incidents into recurring problem types -- a genuine grouping axis. Most samples map soundly (VPN, Email, WiFi->Network), but one clear misclassification: 'Cannot connect to database' is labelled VPN_Connectivity when it should be Database_Issues, contradicting the source text and indicating unreliable extraction."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_frequency_rank",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "This is a deterministic restatement of problem_type's global frequency ordering -- it adds no new analytical axis beyond simply counting problem_type values, so it offers little independent leverage. Groundedness inherits problem_type's errors: the misclassified database row gets rank 1, propagating the same source mismatch."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
  "source_groundedness": 0.6,
  "rationale": "problem_type is highly on-point for identifying recurring incident categories and gives a real grouping axis, though its values show at least one clear source-contradicting misclassification (database issue labelled as VPN). problem_frequency_rank is largely redundant, a mechanical mapping from problem_type's frequency, and inherits the same grounding weakness. Overall the added columns are useful but marred by observable misclassification."
}

